Issue #5: Ensure User Exists Creates Side Effects
Location: Lines 15-20 in lib/usermanagement_utils.robot
```
Current Code:
IF  not ${user_exists}
    Redfish Create User  ${username}  ${OPENBMC_PASSWORD}  ${role}  ${True}
END
```

Issue: Creates user with hardcoded enabled=${True}, never deletes it, no verification of creation success.
Fix: Add cleanup and verification:
```
Ensure User Exists
    [Arguments]  ${username}  ${role}  ${enabled}=${True}

    ${user_exists}=  Run Keyword And Return Status
    ...  Redfish.Get  /redfish/v1/AccountService/Accounts/${username}

    IF  not ${user_exists}
        Redfish Create User  ${username}  ${OPENBMC_PASSWORD}  ${role}  ${enabled}
        # Verify creation succeeded
        ${resp}=  Redfish.Get  /redfish/v1/AccountService/Accounts/${username}
        Should Be Equal As Strings  ${resp.dict['Enabled']}  ${enabled}
        # Mark for cleanup
        Set Test Variable  ${USER_CREATED_BY_TEST}  ${username}
    END
```

Why: Prevents test pollution and allows tests to control initial user state.

Issue #1: Backwards Parameter Logic - Test Names Don't Match Behavior
```
# Test case at line 283
Disable Admin User Verify User Is Not Allowed To Do SSH On SOL Port
    Verify User Enable Disable And SSH Login  admin_user  Administrator  ${HOST_SOL_PORT}  ${False}

# This calls the ELSE branch which ENABLES user first, then disables
# But the test name says "Disable Admin User"
```

Fix Required: The ${user_state} parameter is confusing. It should represent what action to test, not which branch to execute. Rename and refactor:

```
# Option 1: Rename parameter to match actual behavior
Verify User Enable Disable And SSH Login
    [Arguments]  ${username}  ${role}  ${port}  ${test_enable_flow}
    # test_enable_flow=True: test enabling (disable→enable)
    # test_enable_flow=False: test disabling (enable→disable)

# Option 2: Separate into two keywords
Verify User Disable Blocks SSH Access
    [Arguments]  ${username}  ${role}  ${port}
    Set User Account State  ${username}  ${True}
    Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${True}
    Set User Account State  ${username}  ${False}
    Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${False}

Verify User Enable Allows SSH Access
    [Arguments]  ${username}  ${role}  ${port}
    Set User Account State  ${username}  ${False}
    Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${False}
    Set User Account State  ${username}  ${True}
    Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${True}
```

Why: Current logic is backwards - test names don't match what the code actually does. This will cause confusion during maintenance and debugging. The "Disable" test actually starts by enabling the user, and vice versa.

Issue #3: No Verification That State Change Took Effect
```
# Lines 389-393
Set User Account State  ${username}  ${False}
Sleep  ${USER_STATE_WAIT_TIMEOUT}
Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${False}
```

Fix Required:

```
Set User Account State  ${username}  ${False}
# Verify state actually changed via Redfish
Wait Until Keyword Succeeds  ${USER_STATE_WAIT_TIMEOUT}  2s
...  Verify User State Via Redfish  ${username}  ${False}
Verify SSH Login Access  ${username}  ${port}  ${False}

# New keyword needed:
Verify User State Via Redfish
    [Arguments]  ${username}  ${expected_enabled}
    ${resp}=  Redfish.Get  /redfish/v1/AccountService/Accounts/${username}
    Should Be Equal As Strings  ${resp.dict['Enabled']}  ${expected_enabled}
```

Why: Fixed sleep assumes state propagates in exactly 10 seconds. If it takes longer, test fails. If it's faster, we waste time. More critically, we never verify the Redfish PATCH actually worked - BMC could return 200 but fail to apply the change.
